          %h2#idea Idea
          %p
            %em Algebraic topology
            refers to the application of methods of
            %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/algebra"} algebra
            to problems in
            = succeed "." do
              %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/topology"} topology
            = succeed "/" do
              %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/homeomorphism"} homeomorphism
            = succeed "-" do
              %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/homotopy"} homotopy
            %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/invariants"} invariants
            to
            = succeed "," do
              %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/topological+spaces"} topological spaces
            %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/functors"} functors
            from some
            %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/category"} category
            of topological objects (e.g.
            = succeed "," do
              %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/Hausdorff+spaces"} Hausdorff spaces
            = succeed ")" do
              %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/fibre+bundles"} fibre bundles
            = succeed "," do
              %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/abelian+groups"} abelian groups
            %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/modules"} modules
            over the
            = succeed ")." do
              %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/Steenrod+algebra"} Steenrod algebra
          %h3#the_idea_of_functorial_invariants The idea of functorial invariants
          %p
            The basic idea of the functorial method for the problem of existence of morphisms is the following: If
            %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
              %semantics
                %mrow
                  %mi F
                  %mo :
                  %mi A
                  %mo →
                  %mi B
                %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} F:A\to B
            is a
            %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/functor"} functor
            (we present here a general statement, but in the above context
            %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
              %semantics
                %mrow
                  %mi A
                %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} A
            is a category of topological objects and
            %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
              %semantics
                %mrow
                  %mi B
                %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} B
            some category of algebraic objects) and
            %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
              %semantics
                %mrow
                  %mi d
                  %mo :
                  %mi D
                  %mo →
                  %mi A
                %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} d:D\to A
            a
            %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/diagram"} diagram
            in
            %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
              %semantics
                %mrow
                  %mi A
                %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} A
            then
            %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
              %semantics
                %mrow
                  %mi F
                  %mo ∘
                  %mi d
                %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} F\circ d
            is a diagram in
            = succeed "." do
              %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
                %semantics
                  %mrow
                    %mi B
                  %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} B
            %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
              %semantics
                %mrow
                  %mi f
                %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} f
            in the diagram
            %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
              %semantics
                %mrow
                  %mi d
                %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} d
            making the extended diagram commutative, then
            %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
              %semantics
                %mrow
                  %mi F
                  %mo{:stretchy => "false"} (
                  %mi f
                  %mo{:stretchy => "false"} )
                %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} F(f)
            is a morphism between the corresponding vertices in
            %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
              %semantics
                %mrow
                  %mi B
                %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} B
            extending
            %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
              %semantics
                %mrow
                  %mi F
                  %mo ∘
                  %mi d
                %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} F\circ d
            to a commutative diagram. Thus if we prove that there is no morphism extending
            %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
              %semantics
                %mrow
                  %mi F
                  %mo ∘
                  %mi d
                %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} F\circ d
            then there was no morphism extending
            %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
              %semantics
                %mrow
                  %mi d
                %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} d
            in the first place. Therefore, the functorial method is very suitable to prove
            %em negative
            existence for morphisms. Sometimes, however, there is a theorem showing that some set of invariants completely characterizes a problem hence being able to show positive existence or uniqueness for maps or spaces. For the uniqueness for morphisms, it is enough to show that
            %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
              %semantics
                %mrow
                  %mi F
                %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} F
            is faithful and that there is at most one solution for the existence problem in the target category. Faithful functors in this context are rare, but it is sufficient for
            %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
              %semantics
                %mrow
                  %mi F
                %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} F
            to be faithful on some subcategory
            %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
              %semantics
                %mrow
                  %msub
                    %mi A
                    %mi p
                %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} A_p
            of
            %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
              %semantics
                %mrow
                  %mi A
                %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} A
            containing at least all morphisms which are the possible candidates for the solution of the particular existence problem for morphisms.
          %h2#overview_of_methods Overview of methods
          %p
            The archetypical example is the classification of
            %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/surfaces"} surfaces
            via their
            = succeed "." do
              %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/Euler+characteristic"} Euler characteristic
            %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/topological+spaces"} topological spaces
            themselves as rather about the
            %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/homotopy+types"} homotopy types
            which they
            = succeed "." do
              %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/homotopy+hypothesis"} present
            %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/shape+theory"} shape invariants
            for spaces with bad local behaviour.
          %p
            Hence modern algebraic topology is to a large extent the application of algebraic methods to
            = succeed "." do
              %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/homotopy+theory"} homotopy theory
          %p
            A general and powerful such method is the assignment of
            %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/homology"} homology
            and
            %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/cohomology"} cohomology
            %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/groups"} groups
            to topological spaces, such that these
            %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/abelian+groups"} abelian groups
            depend only on the
            = succeed "." do
              %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/homotopy+type"} homotopy type
            %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/ordinary+homology"} ordinary homology
            and
            %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/ordinary+cohomology"} ordinary cohomology
            groups, given by
            = succeed "." do
              %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/singular+simplicial+complexes"} singular simplicial complexes
            = succeed "." do
              %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/homological+algebra"} homological algebra
          %p
            The
            %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/axiom"} axiomatization
            of the properties of such
            %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/cohomology"} cohomology
            group assignments is what led to the formulation of the trinity of concepts of
            = succeed "," do
              %em
                %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/category"} category
            %em
              %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/functor"} functor
            and
            = succeed "," do
              %em
                %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/natural+transformations"} natural transformations
            = succeed "." do
              %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/category+theory"} category theory
          %p
            In particular this leads to the formulation of
            %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/generalized+%28Eilenberg-Steenrod%29+cohomology"} generalized (Eilenberg-Steenrod) cohomology
            theories which detect more information about classes of homotopy types. By the
            %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/Brown+representability+theorem"} Brown representability theorem
            such are represented by
            %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/spectra"} spectra
            (generalizing
            = succeed ")," do
              %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/chain+complexes"} chain complexes
            = succeed "," do
              %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/stable+homotopy+types"} stable homotopy types
            = succeed "." do
              %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/stable+homotopy+theory"} stable homotopy theory
          %p
            Still finer invariants of
            %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/homotopy+types"} homotopy types
            are detected by further refinements of these “algebraic” structures, for instance to
            = succeed "," do
              %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/multiplicative+cohomology+theories"} multiplicative cohomology theories
            = succeed "/" do
              %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/equivariant+homotopy+theory"} equivariant homotopy theory
            %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/equivariant+stable+homotopy+theory"} equivariant stable homotopy theory
            and so forth. The construction and analysis of these requires the intimate combination of algebra and homotopy theory to
            %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/higher+category+theory"} higher category theory
            and
            = succeed "," do
              %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/higher+algebra"} higher algebra
            %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/universal+algebra"} universal
            higher algebra of
            = succeed "." do
              %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/operads"} operads
          %p
            The central tool for breaking down all this
            %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/higher+algebra"} higher algebraic
            data into computable pieces are
            = succeed "," do
              %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/spectral+sequences"} spectral sequences
